---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro'
import Nav from '../../components/sections/Nav.astro'
import Footer from '../../components/sections/Footer.astro'
import SeriesNav from '../../components/blog/SeriesNav.astro'
import HundredYearHero from '../../components/blog/HundredYearHero.astro'
import Byline from '../../components/blog/Byline.astro'
import { postBySlug, formatPostDate } from '../../data/blog'

const post = postBySlug('the-hundred-year-machine')!
---

<Base title={`${post.title} — xNet`} description={post.description}>
  <Nav />

  <main>
    <HundredYearHero
      title={post.title}
      deck={post.description}
      date={formatPostDate(post.pubDate)}
      readingMinutes={post.readingMinutes}
      tags={post.tags}
    />

    <article
      class="prose prose-lg mx-auto max-w-3xl px-6 py-16 dark:prose-invert prose-headings:tracking-tight prose-a:text-indigo-600 dark:prose-a:text-indigo-400"
    >
      <Byline post={post} />

      <p>
        Ester Strogen was eighty-two when her family worked out that she was
        renting her telephones. Two black rotary phones, the kind you dial
        with a finger, leased since the 1960s and still costing her $29.10 a
        month in 2006. Over about forty-two years her family reckons she paid
        more than $14,000 for equipment worth perhaps a hundred dollars.
      </p>
      <p>
        Here is the part that stops the story being a joke at one company's
        expense. By the standards of her century, this was normal. For most
        of the twentieth century an American could not own a telephone. The
        handset was the phone company's property, leased with the line, and
        plugging in your own was forbidden. A regulator finally opened the
        network to customer-owned equipment in 1968, but the leases outlived
        the reasoning by decades. She was far from alone, either: in 2007 a
        successor company still had around 580,000 people paying monthly rent
        on telephones.
      </p>
      <p>
        Sit with why this reads as absurd. Nobody rents a kettle. The story
        only works as a scandal because somewhere along the way we all
        agreed, without ever saying it aloud, that a household machine is a
        thing you own. I want to trace where that agreement came from.
        We are quietly tearing it up.
      </p>

      <h2 id="the-apartment">Eight o'clock in the apartment</h2>

      <p>
        There is a website I keep going back to called
        <a href="https://ordinaryabundance.com" rel="noopener noreferrer"
          >Ordinary Abundance</a
        >, built by a researcher named Jordan Dworkin. It walks you through a
        modern apartment over the course of an evening. At eight o'clock the
        music is playing softly over the speaker; you pick up a book, flick
        on the lamp, settle into the chair. At nine you wander into the
        kitchen, rifle through the fruit bowl, open and close the fridge,
        put the kettle on. At ten you wash down a pill, brush your teeth,
        and at eleven you switch off the lights and go to bed.
      </p>
      <p>
        Each ordinary object opens a voice from history, someone for whom
        that object was the edge of the imaginable. In 1888, Edward Bellamy's
        narrator calls music at home "the limit of human felicity": "perfect
        in quality, unlimited in quantity, suited to every mood, and
        beginning and ceasing at will". A witness in Wabash, Indiana, one of the
        first towns lit by electric light, describes a crowd standing
        "overwhelmed with awe, as if in the presence of the supernatural".
        When piped water reached New York in 1842, a diarist recorded the
        whole city shouting one word: "Water! Water!" The arrival of New
        England pond-ice in Calcutta moved a newspaper to rank the shippers
        with "other benefactors of mankind".
      </p>
      <p>
        Its argument is about gratitude. These things were miracles;
        familiarity has made them invisible; it serves us, and honours the
        people who built them, to feel the awe again on purpose. I think
        that argument is right. And I want to add a second one to it,
        because the tour shows something else that Dworkin never quite
        names. Nearly every object in that apartment has the same
        biography.
      </p>

      <h2 id="the-second-walk">The second walk</h2>

      <p>
        Start at the fruit bowl. The tour's kitchen pauses on the pineapple,
        with Charles Lamb in 1822 working himself into a fever about its
        taste. What it mentions gently, and what floors me, is how the fruit
        was handled a century earlier. An English-grown pineapple cost so
        much that people did not eat them. Hostesses <em>rented</em> a
        pineapple for the evening, displayed it as the centrepiece, and
        handed it back. That same fruit toured from party to party until it
        began to rot, and only then did somebody finally buy and eat it. A
        rented pineapple sounds like satire. It was simply what the miracle
        cost when it was new.
      </p>
      <p>
        Then hothouses and steamships did their work, and the miracle
        finished its journey: from spectacle, to service, to a thing in a
        bowl you own outright and eat without thinking.
      </p>
      <p>
        So did the cold in the fridge. Within two generations, that pond-ice
        crossing the ocean to Calcutta became the iceman on his route, and
        the ice arrived as a subscription. A card in the window told him how
        many pounds to carry up; the service came daily because the cold ran
        out daily. Then the electric refrigerator ended all of it — the
        route, the window cards, the whole daily relationship collapsed into
        a white box, bought once, that made its own cold. Music too: Bellamy
        could only imagine his endless music as a service, piped down wires
        from central halls, because that was what luxury looked like from
        1888. What actually arrived was the record, and the shelf of them in
        the sitting room became the standard way a family owned its music
        for a hundred years.
      </p>
      <p>
        That is the shape. Once you see it, you see it on every wall of
        the apartment. A miracle arrives as a service for the rich, with
        attendants: the concert, the iceman, the water-carrier, the rented
        pineapple. Then it matures into an object that ordinary people own
        outright, and the attendants disappear. It happened so reliably that
        only a monopoly could stop it: the telephone stayed leased for a
        century because Bell stood in the doorway. Ester Strogen's $14,000
        rotary phone is what a blocked arc looks like, and we recognise her
        story as a scandal precisely because everything else in the room
        made it through.
      </p>

      <h2 id="the-film-backwards">The film runs backwards</h2>

      <p>
        Now look at the newest objects in the apartment. The film is
        running the other way.
      </p>
      <p>
        Music got there first, which is fitting, since it led the march in
        both directions. Records became downloads, downloads became the
        stream, and the stream is Bellamy's wire service again, right down
        to the monthly bill. Dworkin's sitting room has a detail I find quietly
        devastating: the music playing at eight o'clock is "a playlist your
        friend made for you years ago". That playlist is a real thing your
        friend made, with care, and it exists only for as long as one
        company keeps one database answering. Stop paying, or let the
        company stumble, and the miracle switches off in a way no record
        shelf ever could. Software followed. Photoshop was a box you bought
        and owned for a decade; in 2013 it became a seat you rent by the
        month, and the industry took notes.
      </p>
      <p>
        Devices came last, and they are the widow's telephones with better
        industrial design. Revolv sold a smart-home hub with a "lifetime
        subscription"; after Google acquired the company it published a
        date, and past that date, in its own words, "your Revolv hub and app
        will no longer work". The lifetime turned out to be the product's.
        Insteon is the cleaner case. There is no villain in it at all.
        That smart-home firm simply ran out of money in the spring of
        2022, and its servers went quiet. Hubs in tens of thousands of
        houses went dark the same week, because nobody had designed them to
        work without a company attached. Spotify sold a little dashboard
        music player called Car Thing, discontinued it, and switched the
        existing ones off by remote, offering refunds only after complaints
        and a lawsuit.
      </p>
      <p>
        So walk the apartment one last time, asking each object the question
        the widow's family never got to ask. If every company that made you
        vanished tonight, what still works in the morning? The answers sort
        the room with embarrassing speed. The bulb burns, indifferent to who
        is still trading; the tap runs, the fridge hums, the bicycle rolls,
        the spectacles focus, and a book stays a book. A treadle Singer
        sewing machine from before the First World War will still sew you a
        shirt today, which is where this essay gets its title. And the
        speaker falls silent, the playlist evaporates, the hub bricks, and
        whatever lived only as an account dies with its landlord.
      </p>
      <p>
        Notice where the line falls, because it matters more than anything
        else in this essay: not between physical things and digital ones. A
        file on your own disk survives its maker as surely as the Singer
        does. Lawyers have a name for what actually changed; two of them,
        Aaron Perzanowski and Jason Schultz, wrote a book called
        <em>The End of Ownership</em> about the moment buying quietly became
        licensing. The split is architectural. A machine that carries its
        works inside itself belongs to you, and a machine that leaves its
        works at the vendor's house is a terminal, whatever the receipt
        says. A terminal is rented even when you are told you bought it.
      </p>

      <h2 id="the-receipt">The receipt</h2>

      <p>
        I should turn the mirror around before I say anything else, because
        we charge a monthly fee too. xNet Cloud runs servers for people who
        would rather not run their own, and it bills for that every month.
        So you will get no sermon from me about subscriptions being wicked.
        The iceman charged monthly as well, and he was charging honestly:
        the money bought his labour, his horse, his route, an operation that
        genuinely ran every day. What matters was never whether money
        changes hands on a schedule; it is what the money buys. A fee for
        work someone actually does is a service, while a fee for continued
        access to what you would otherwise simply own is rent, and the whole
        history above is the history of rent losing.
      </p>
      <p>
        Our <a
          href="https://github.com/crs48/xNet/blob/main/docs/CHARTER.md"
          rel="noopener noreferrer">Charter</a
        > tries to hold that line with a set of written tests, and the one
        that belongs to this essay is called the vanish test: "if
        xNet‑the‑company disappeared tomorrow, what the customer paid for
        (their data, their audience, their workflows) survives." Insteon is
        the vanish test failed; the Singer is the same test passed and held
        for a century.
      </p>
      <p>
        Passing it is not a mood; it is a set of build decisions, each one
        checkable. Your master copy lives on your own disk, and the app
        works with no server at all. Export is free, complete and verified,
        so leaving costs nothing but the decision. The protocol and the hub
        are MIT-licensed, which means the company dying does not take the
        software's legal right to exist with it. Old protocol versions are
        deprecated rather than cut off, so a client that worked yesterday is
        not made a brick by an update it never asked for. And there is a
        promise in the Charter I have grown fonder of than any of the grand
        ones: the floor. We name the oldest machine the app must run well
        on, a 2017-class laptop with 8 GB of memory, and a gate in our
        build system fails any change that quietly raises it. On why that
        is the only claim of its kind we make, the Charter is blunt:
        "'Greener' would be marketing. 'Your old laptop keeps working' is
        measurable, so that is the only claim we make."
      </p>
      <p>
        The widow never got a receipt like that. Nobody offering her the
        lease ever had to write down what would happen to her telephones if
        the company lost interest, because the answer was understood: they
        were never hers. Modern software mostly runs on the same
        understanding, wrapped in a nicer onboarding flow. It seems little
        enough to ask that the promise be written where a build system can
        enforce it, rather than where a marketing team can.
      </p>

      <h2 id="ordinary">Ordinary</h2>

      <p>
        Dworkin ends his tour at eleven o'clock, with the lights off and a
        head on a pillow, and closes with the thought that carries the whole
        site: our lives are "a gallery of past generations' heroic efforts",
        and it serves us, and honours them, to feel the old awe on purpose.
      </p>
      <p>
        I would only add the condition that makes his title work. A miracle
        becomes <em>ordinary</em> abundance at a precise moment: when you no
        longer have to think about it. And you only ever stop thinking about
        a thing that cannot be taken back. Nobody checks the tap's terms of
        service before filling the kettle, or wonders, lifting the fridge
        door handle, whether the account is in good standing. That
        unthinking trust is not complacency. It is the finish line. The
        pineapple crossed it, and the ice, and the record — every machine in
        that apartment except the newest ones, which have been marched back
        to the start and handed a monthly invoice.
      </p>
      <p>
        The awe Dworkin wants us to recover is deserved. So is the
        arrangement our great-grandparents fought their way to, one machine
        at a time, and gave to us so completely that we forgot it was ever
        won. The treadle Singer in the corner has been holding the standard
        for over a hundred years without a server, a licence check, or a
        renewal email. That is not nostalgia; it is a spec, and software
        written by people who mean it can still be built to meet it — on
        your own disk, on your old laptop, for as long as you care to keep
        it, whatever becomes of us.
      </p>

      <hr />

